## Interpreting the comparison

Use a row that resembles your application. Rendering one preview, updating an existing engine and starting a CLI for each file incur different costs. Successful artifact and label checks cover the features asserted for those inputs. Visual fidelity, clipping, accessibility and interaction need their own checks. A performance difference between products can suggest an experiment. Establish its cause by comparing Merman revisions on the same source, settings and output contract.

For native Rust peers and comparisons between Merman revisions, consult the dedicated measurements. [Native Merman and mmdr comparison](https://github.com/Latias94/merman/blob/main/docs/performance/renderer_comparison_2026-07-28_75c9fd156_vs_mmdr.md) · [Same-implementation release evidence](https://github.com/Latias94/merman/blob/main/docs/release/RELEASE_REPORTS.md)

## Reproduce this report

The [portable measurements]($receipt) contain the sample units, workload fingerprints and observations used here. To regenerate this Markdown from the adjacent receipt, run the following from a checkout that contains the report generator.

```console
python tools/bench/perf_runner.py workloads report --from path/to/measurements.json
```

Use the path of the linked JSON in place of `path/to/measurements.json`. Add `--check` to verify the adjacent Markdown. The JSON records the plan and product fingerprints for each measurement. $raw_bundle

See the [measurement contract](https://github.com/Latias94/merman/blob/main/docs/performance/BENCHMARKING.md) and [collection and publication procedure](https://github.com/Latias94/merman/blob/main/docs/performance/RUNBOOK.md#publish-a-reader-report) to repeat the measurements with a new build.
